WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.361
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.361 --> 00:00:07.547
It's cold and dark.

00:00:07.547 --> 00:00:10.528
A bitter, unrelenting snow surrounds you.

00:00:10.528 --> 00:00:15.910
It's three A.M., and you've been walking
through downtown Boston for over an hour.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.670
You went to MIT, and you launched a
successful start-up.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:21.590
But, that's over now.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:27.670
And all you can ask yourself is, how did I
end up here?

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.780
You don't know and you can't sleep.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.620
So you walk that frigid two and a half
miles in the middle of the night.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.704
This is my life in November 2012.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:44.540
I spent two years in the trenches building
my first real start-up.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.900
Finally tasted success only to watch it
drift away.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:53.510
I had learned what it meant to rise and
now I was learning what it means to fall.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:01:00.420
Abraham Lincoln said that we are greedy to
publish the success of our efforts but

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.780
shy as to publishing our failures.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.310
And that we are ruined by this one-sided
practice of concealment

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.670
of blunders and failures.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.900
For me, that ends today.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.850
This is a talk about falling.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.380
One night in 1999, my family and I were
about to drive back home from dinner.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.088
I was busy playing one of the greatest
games of all time.

00:01:24.088 --> 00:01:25.924
Pokemon red.

00:01:25.924 --> 00:01:28.040
[LAUGH] It's a great game.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:29.950
I got a new Pokemon.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:30.690
I was so excited.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:31.620
I ran to tell my mom.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.970
But, I didn't quite make it.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.010
See, I don't remember seeing the curb in
the parking lot that night.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:42.743
But I distinctly recall the taste of
cement when [SOUND] my face hit

00:01:42.743 --> 00:01:43.715
the ground.

00:01:43.715 --> 00:01:44.643
Falling is.

00:01:44.643 --> 00:01:46.384
Painful.

00:01:46.384 --> 00:01:51.314
My mom picked me up and saw that one of my
two front teeth had been pushed down,

00:01:51.314 --> 00:01:56.135
and was now the better part of two
millimeters south of where it should be.

00:01:56.135 --> 00:01:57.740
[LAUGH] Not good.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.340
We rushed to the emergency room.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:04.540
They told us to call the dentist, and he
said, I can see you in the morning, but

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.320
by then it'll be too late.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.810
If you wanna fix this, you're going to
push it,

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.020
you're gonna have to push it back up
yourself.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.730
I was 10, but these were permanent teeth.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.055
And if you feel queasy right now, imagine
how I felt.

00:02:18.055 --> 00:02:21.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I grabbed my mom's hands and

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.510
squeezed as hard as I could.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.420
Crying, pushing it one.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.232
Two millimeters back into place and it
worked.

00:02:30.232 --> 00:02:31.207
See?

00:02:31.207 --> 00:02:36.040
[LAUGH]
Sometimes falling isn't a choice,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.458
but getting back up is.

00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:41.610
Investors in start-ups often say that one
of the biggest risks in

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.290
early stage companies.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.690
Is the team.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.980
I couldn't agree more.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:47.910
Let's go back to 2012.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.360
It was a bright day in August and I went
for

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.270
a walk with my co-founder, as I often did.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.880
But, this time, something wasn't quite
right.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.800
The summer had been a rollercoaster.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.539
Ups and downs.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.310
And I could just feel it as we were
walking.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.120
Something was very wrong.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.650
We sat down to talk and I looked at him
and said what I could not believe but

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:13.440
knew to be true.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:18.640
Look, if you're gonna fire me, just do it.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:19.140
And he did.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:21.850
We'd started this together.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:23.680
And he fired me.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.480
I went back to the office that night to
get my things, and, I looked around and

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.470
saw the company I had helped build, and
the products I helped create.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:36.840
And in that moment, I felt like I had lost
part of myself.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.860
Leaving the start-up was complicated.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.600
We both had leverage and we began to
negotiate.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.500
I'd never done this before.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.920
And I made just about every mistake in the
book.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.650
Every time we'd come close to reaching an
agreement.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:59.950
I'd just be overtaken by fear, and ask for
something new, something more.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.780
What should have taken weeks turned into
months.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.770
I was winning the negotiation, mind you.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.310
But at the same time I was losing what was
left of our friendship.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.130
I felt terrible but I couldn't find a way
out.

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.720
And by Thanksgiving I was burnt out.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:18.820
I was done.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:27.240
What had started as a simple negotiation,
had become a chronic illness with no cure.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.910
But sometimes it's our darkest moments
that provide us with the greatest clarity.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:37.960
And from that low vantage point, it all
made sense.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.510
I had become so afraid.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.140
In Boston, I hadn't just lost my start-up.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.010
I had lost myself.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.400
I had forgotten about the future.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:53.260
I knew then that I had to stop trying to
win.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:55.490
So that's what I did.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.389
I forgave my friend and I forgave myself.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.390
Fewer shares would be enough.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.480
This changed everything between us.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.580
And in that moment, we became old friends
again.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.420
And he even agreed to help me sell enough
shares to take a year off.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.925
You know, it was one of those win, win
scenarios I've always heard about.

00:05:18.925 --> 00:05:21.610
[LAUGH] They actually do exist.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.820
The process wasn't easy, but by the end of
2012, I was free.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.160
You know,

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.120
my greatest fear in leaving the start up
was not the money or my reputation.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.690
My greatest fear was becoming a guy named
Pete Best.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.930
Now, Pete Best was the first, the
original.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:45.180
Drummer for The Beatles, but you may not
have heard of him.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.480
Not because he missed out, but because
shortly after leaving The Beatles,

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:52.740
he didn't play a single show for 20 years.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.730
Steve Jobs was fired from Apple only after
making the cover of Time Magazine,

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.750
but because he kept growing and learning.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:06.360
He ended up on the cover of not one, but
five more times.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:08.336
I had a choice to make.

00:06:08.336 --> 00:06:09.640
Would I keep growing?

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:14.000
Would I keep learning?

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.250
In my year off I moved to Austin, Texas to
build a live and

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.660
work design studio with my friends.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:23.280
It was awesome, and Austin is an
incredible place.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.770
I chose, I chose Austin because it, it's
the city where

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:34.790
it is whatever you need it to be and it
would be my place to recharge.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.680
We ended up using the studio as this
incredible retreat and

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.050
we invited all kinds of people down to
visit us.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.680
Entrepreneurs, musicians, engineers,
screen writers and

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:46.730
even a National Spelling Bee Champion
came.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.474
It was awesome.

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:50.791
Seriously there aren't that many.

00:06:50.791 --> 00:06:56.190
[LAUGH] But, you know we together we
talked about falling and

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.200
what it takes to get back up again and
again.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.400
I discovered that this is called grit.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.070
University of Pennsylvania professor
Angela Duckworth

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:08.090
defines grit as perseverance and passion
for long term goals.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:12.920
She says that these goals can take years,
even decades to complete.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:18.650
And her research shows that this requires
unwavering focus.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:19.680
But wait a minute, wait a minute,.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.040
If being gritty means sticking with your
goals,

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.140
then what about leaving the start-up.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.110
How do these things match up?

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.040
So when I got the chance to interview her,
I asked about flexibility.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:39.000
Professor Duckworth told me that less
gritty people have it backwards.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.770
They're a little too flexible about their
long term goals.

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:46.330
And at the same time, too rigid about how
they get there day to day.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:52.130
Gritty people, on the other hand, are the
complete and total opposite.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:54.120
Dead set on their long term vision and

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.650
at the same time pretty flexible about how
it happens.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:03.480
When you know where you want to end up,
exactly which path you take.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.490
Is less important.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.410
On my path, I had fallen.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:12.159
And though my time in Austin was an
absolute gift,

00:08:12.159 --> 00:08:15.031
I knew that I couldn't rest forever.

00:08:15.031 --> 00:08:18.772
I came to Stanford to learn about the
future of hardware.

00:08:18.772 --> 00:08:23.054
I came to Stanford to learn about how to
start consumer brands that enchant

00:08:23.054 --> 00:08:23.754
the world.

00:08:23.754 --> 00:08:26.170
I came to Stanford to get.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:26.890
Back up.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:34.960
And as you walk on your path, I can almost
guarantee that you will fall and

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:40.210
that it will hurt, but remember that it's
okay to be flexible.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:43.670
And sometimes, you know, it's not just
okay, it's necessary.

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:48.390
Grit is having the patience and the
tenacity to keep after it.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:51.400
Until you can get there, no matter which
way you end up going.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:59.730
When you fall, and when it hurts, and when
that path grows dark.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:04.636
In that moment of doubt and pain, remember
what I have come to know deeply.

00:09:04.636 --> 00:09:08.358
Your future, our future.

00:09:08.358 --> 00:09:11.049
Is brighter than the darkness.

00:09:11.049 --> 00:09:11.779
Thank you.

00:09:11.779 --> 00:09:16.841
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:16.841 --> 00:09:21.589
[MUSIC]

